prompt Autonomous loop: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; each lap deep-read one council-rota doc; Rye prioritized; send often; stop at custody gates.
think Read REMEMBER, the eight-season double-seat, the 1024 itinerary head, and the seed. The live edge is the open-media font family; the five accent corpora (acute/grave/circumflex/tilde/diaeresis) just completed the vowel-diacritic taxonomy.
think Surveyed the Latin-1 Supplement lowercase block: every accented vowel is drawn, yet three ALPHABETIC letters remain undrawn -- a ring (U+00E5), ae ligature (U+00E6), o stroke (U+00F8) -- the vowels a Danish or Norwegian alphabet appends after z. Drawing them closes the lowercase Latin-1 letter set, a strong Lindy completion.
think Chose these three as one coherent corpus, deferring the Icelandic letters (eth/thorn/y acute) and the a-ring capital to later laps (SLC: ship one whole tight thing). The crux is that these are NOT one shared mark but three distinct constructions -- a hollow ring above, a mark-less ligature with a crossbar, an o with a stroke through the bowl.
think Matched the diaeresis corpus's exact shape: ASCII line-art specification, coverage lifted from the art, gfa1 round-trip, present/distinct/legible, plus three per-letter toothed guards rather than one shared placement check.
obs Designed the ring as a 2-row hollow mark (top bar over two sides, blank center) atop the acute a body; the ae as a wide fused pair with a full row-4 crossbar; the o stroke as an anti-diagonal slash inking the bowl center where a plain o is hollow. Ink landed 15..19 of 35, GREEN on the first metal run, no red.
obs tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII though the glyphs cover a non-ASCII range (numeric codepoint literals -- the ascii-first exception for a font codec's glyph tables).
obs Rota lap deep-read item 1 (foundations/README.md) -- the why beneath the how; this corpus is Lindy-durable floor code, a whole bounded thing proven on metal and sent as its own signed round.
